1
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:06,380
Imagine the year 2050. Most diseases of today are treatable.

2
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:14,350
But as we go, all that once still remains a threat.

3
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:15,340
cancer.

4
00:00:15,910 --> 00:00:18,930
Already it is the second leading cause of death worldwide.

5
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:25,740
Recently, cell therapies like CAR T cells have revolutionized the treatment of blood cancers.

6
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:28,300
Yet they remained far from perfect.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,990
They resist against solid tumors, which account for over 90% of all cancer cases.

8
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:40,210
Within the tumor microenvironment, immunosuppressive signals exhaust car T cells

9
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:41,870
and block efficacy.

10
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:47,020
So we went to the drawing board. What would the perfect cell therapy look like?

11
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:51,860
off the shelf, safe and capable of targeting all tumors.

12
00:00:52,450 --> 00:01:01,330
We envision a bottom-up synthetic immune cell designed for tumor-specific sensing, reversible signal processing, and flexible responses.

13
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:06,900
To test our vision, we entered Heidelberg's largest innovation competition.

14
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:13,260
Winning it highlighted the potential of our idea motivated us to bring Phoenix to life.

15
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:21,070
First, we engineered a sensing layer targeting tumor-specific ligands and activating our cells.

16
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:27,280
To ensure safety, we added a second receptor detecting healthy tissue cues, providing an off switch.

17
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:34,670
With the help of our digital twin Spark, we use coarse-grained simulations to reduce the background activity of our receptor constructs.

18
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:40,880
Next, once a ligand is detected, the cell must process the signal quickly and reversibly.

19
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:46,610
For this, we engineered a dynamic phosphorylation circuit inspired by T cell logic.

20
00:01:47,330 --> 00:01:54,640
spark models these biochemical processes in CD-core and allows us to quickly identify optimal circuit compositions

21
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:56,210
for fine-tuned activity.

22
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:00,660
Finally, molecular signals trigger therapeutic response.

23
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:08,660
The FOSFO binder, our output module, enables flexible control over immune-like functions such as expression,

24
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:12,750
or secretion of therapeutic proteins like interleukin-12.

25
00:02:13,570 --> 00:02:15,120
But there is one more thing.

26
00:02:15,910 --> 00:02:19,630
How do we depth sensing without having to rebuild the entire unit?

27
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:21,230
We use Spark.

28
00:02:21,610 --> 00:02:24,560
to create de novo binders to cancer proteins.

29
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:31,020
Next, molecular dynamic simulations and wet lab validation identify functional binders.

30
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:34,510
This can now be used to create custom receptors.

31
00:02:35,170 --> 00:02:37,620
We applied this pipeline to GDF 15.

32
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,910
A protein are regulated in pancreatic cancer.

33
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:44,590
and post-wiki freeze, we created a functioning receptor.

34
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:51,790
The binding of GDF15 recruits the substrate which leads to phosphorylation and nano-luciferase expression.

35
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:53,420
To our excitement.

36
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:58,290
We measured significant ligand-dependent increase on the first try.

37
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:04,560
This proves that with Spark we can rapidly retarget Phoenix cells to any cancer protein.

38
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:10,320
Sensing, processing, responding.

39
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:21,010
Together, these modules formed the Phoenix Toolbox, an interchangeable and expandable system developed with input from oncologists, protein engineers, and translational scientists.

40
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:29,520
To make our collection even better, we are currently extending our sensing repertoire to include pH, ATP, succinate and hypoxia.

41
00:03:30,370 --> 00:03:37,620
To guide researchers and IGMAs in building synthetic cells, SPARK predicts how receptors, enzymes and substrates behave.

42
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:44,530
The result is a versatile framework for programmable immunity, ready to be deployed in a variety of chassis cells.

43
00:03:46,090 --> 00:03:50,930
Bringing the proof of concept to the clinic is something students like us are barely trained for.

44
00:03:51,330 --> 00:04:01,460
So together with experts in regulation, translation, and patient advocacy, we hosted a panel discussion where students learned how research can truly reach patients.

45
00:04:02,050 --> 00:04:08,240
This led us to develop the Heidelberg Translation Roadmap, a student's guide navigating the innovation network.

46
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:13,460
It clarifies who to contact, when to engage, and how to prepare.

47
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:21,070
After the JEMBRE, our next JEM Forum will ignite the connections between young scientists and stakeholders.

48
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:25,230
With this foundation, we're ready to look ahead.

49
00:04:25,930 --> 00:04:27,380
Let's fast forward 9 years.

50
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:30,640
patient is diagnosed with pancreatic cancer.

51
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:41,300
After a biopsy and secretome analysis, we identified cancer tumor characteristic proteins and generate a personalized Phoenix cell within days.

52
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:44,940
That same design now joins an off-the-shelf library.

53
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:49,100
that match the tumor profile of the next patient.

